**The Latency Breakdown:**
1.  **Prompt Processing:**
    *   VRAM Bandwidth: Fast.
    *   System RAM Bandwidth: 4 GB transfer.
    *   Time: $4 \text{GB} / 50 \text{GB/s} \approx 80 \text{ms}$.
2.  **Decode Phase (Start):**
    *   KV Cache Traffic: 16 GB.
    *   System RAM Bandwidth: 16 GB transfer.
    *   Time: $16 \text{GB} / 50 \text{GB/s} \approx 320 \text{ms}$.
    *   *Note:* At this point, the GPU has no VRAM left to hold the next token's KV cache. It must swap to System RAM.

**Dominance Analysis:**
If the system RAM is the bottleneck during the decode phase (which it is, as it has to swap to System RAM first), then:
*   **Total Time (End of Generation):** $80 \text{ms} (\text{Prefill}) + 320 \text{ms} (\text{Decode}) \approx 400 \text{ms}$.
*   **Average Latency:** $400 \text{ms} / 250 \text{tokens} \approx 1.6 \text{ms/token}$.

**Conclusion:**
Even though the *peak* throughput is excellent (tokens/sec is high because the GPU is busy), the **interactive latency** is dominated by the decode phase because the system RAM is full. The user experiences high TPS but slow interaction. Increasing context to 8,000 tokens would double the KV cache to 32 GB.
*   If 32 GB VRAM is the limit, the decode phase is already saturated (swapping to System RAM is the final step).
*   If 32 GB System RAM is also the limit, the system runs out of memory entirely, crashing.
*   In this specific case, the "excellent throughput" was a false positive; the real bottleneck is the **System RAM bandwidth**, not the GPU. The system is waiting for the 32 GB of System RAM to fill up before it can process the next token.

